ux of the sci-fi  setup is that among other things the  halcyon system is owned and run entirely  by a board of corporations and their  presence is a big deal  corporate capitalism so deeply affects  everything in the outer worlds and  explorations into how it can affect  society on a variety of levels it's a  surprisingly well considered constant  despite the semblance of parody you'll  meet sympathetic workers whose  livelihood  made possible by offering themselves to  exploitation in indentured servitude  white collared outlaws who are more  bureaucrats than pirates and  well-meaning middle managers who are  just trying to change the corporate  machine from the inside you'll find  moderates idealists extremists and most  things in between and around the fringes  all of whom have their own feasible  ideas about how to best serve the colony  or themselves by the time the climax  hits it's clear that the outer world has  its own stance on this bleak future but  that doesn't stop the world that creates  the surgeons you take and characters you  meet along the way from being any less  fascinating now there are plenty of  characters in the outer worlds who I  didn't like Reed Thompson for example is  a sniveling factory chief in the early  hours of the game who I didn't have to  think twice about undermining and Felix  one of your potential companion  characters had such an annoyingly naive  personality I avoided talking to him as  much as possible now the outer world  allows you to kill any character in the  game bar one and the world will reshape  and move on without them but there's  something to be said for the game's  depiction of its unappealing people  whose portrayal I admire despite my  distaste I never felt like I'd to enjoy  stretches of pointless or overly  dramatic exchanges both because of how  focused and subtle the script seemed to  be as well as the variety of response  options for my play character which kept  conversations flowing in largely natural 